WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:05.590
Film Courage: Does that deferring and delaying
and awkwardness show the mark of a professional?

00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:10.879
UCLA Screenwriting chair Richard Walter: Yes
absolutely.

00:00:10.879 --> 00:00:18.060
You show me a writer who says she was satisfied
with what she did and that it went well I’ll

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.920
show you an amateur.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:23.439
Film Courage: So in the beginning it’s like
dating, there’s a honeymoon phase, everybody

00:00:23.439 --> 00:00:24.439
looks perfect.

00:00:24.439 --> 00:00:27.640
Richard Walter: Well dating is better.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:33.630
If everything works out eventually you get
on well with that person but you never get

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:36.150
along well with writing.

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:37.930
That never changes.

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:44.680
Maybe gosh-willing you are able to make money
at it and actually support yourself at it.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:48.260
But it’s always, always painful, always
a struggle.

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:50.730
And again it’s about feeling, that’s what
it’s about.

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:55.910
It sort of makes sense that it should be feelingful.

00:00:55.910 --> 00:01:00.390
Film Courage: When does a writer know it’s
time to abandon a character?

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:02.550
Richard Walter: You never really know.

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:07.260
And I’m asked that all the time about…I’m
asked two kind of questions all the time about

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:08.260
that.

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:09.600
What I’m called the macro version and the
micro version.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:17.061
The micro version is should I…you read this
script, you read several versions of it, am

00:01:17.061 --> 00:01:21.530
I beating it too much, should I just move
on?

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:22.690
I sure don’t know?

00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:27.520
I’ve seen people rewrite scripts into oblivion
where they should have gotten rid of in my

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.620
view.

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:36.960
And I’ve also seen conversely scripts that
look pretty hopeless that suddenly broke through.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:38.390
Who am I to say?

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:39.390
I can’t tell you that.

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:41.659
But you’ve got to do all of the work on
that.

00:01:41.659 --> 00:01:45.360
That’s the micro version “Should I stick
with this script or should I give it up?”

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:53.260
I’ll also say what is very useful is to
put a script aside and write something else

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:58.940
and then come back to it.

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:04.619
The macro version of it is (and I get this
all of the time) should I…do I have what

00:02:04.619 --> 00:02:06.130
it takes to be a writer?

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:09.079
Am I good enough at this?

00:02:09.079 --> 00:02:10.899
And my answer is I don’t know?

00:02:10.899 --> 00:02:15.980
That’s not for me to say?

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:18.840
Should I continue to do this?

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:24.459
Actually my answer to this is usually no,
don’t continue to do this.

00:02:24.459 --> 00:02:28.980
Because you had to ask me if you should do
it.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:33.529
If you need to ask somebody as to whether
or not you should be a writer then you shouldn’t

00:02:33.529 --> 00:02:34.529
be a writer?

00:02:34.529 --> 00:02:37.870
You should be a writer because you can’t
help yourself, there is nothing else you can

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:38.980
do.

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:42.349
You just can’t stop yourself, you have to
write.

00:02:42.349 --> 00:02:44.959
Not because it’s fun, but because you have
to do it.

00:02:44.959 --> 00:02:50.069
Because you have to breath you know?

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:54.739
As I said we were in the midst of our admissions
process here at UCLA.

00:02:54.739 --> 00:02:59.670
The “take rate,” that is to say the number
of people who are admitted to the program

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:01.870
who actually enter the program is very high.

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:04.209
It’s almost 90%…it’s almost 100%.

00:03:04.209 --> 00:03:07.480
It’s over 90%.

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:11.019
That is to say anybody who is admitted to
the program comes into the program for the

00:03:11.019 --> 00:03:12.019
most part.

00:03:12.019 --> 00:03:17.120
It’s extremely unusual that someone is admitted
to the program and has doubts about whether

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:19.099
they want to enter the program.

00:03:19.099 --> 00:03:25.900
And yet I met with somebody the other day
who had been admitted to the program and now

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:31.830
told me that he was wondering if he should
enter the program and he wanted to ask me

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:38.329
some question about that and I said to him
"Look are you asking me should you enter this

00:03:38.329 --> 00:03:39.329
program?”

00:03:39.329 --> 00:03:43.980
And the way he put it to me “Is this is
reasonable, intelligent thing for me to do?”

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:47.139
And I told him “No!

00:03:47.139 --> 00:03:48.419
No!

00:03:48.419 --> 00:03:52.980
Is it reasonable and intelligent to think
that you can create characters and stories

00:03:52.980 --> 00:04:00.810
and make up dialogue that they speak and that
people will line up in bad weather to pay

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:07.150
over $14.00 or $16.00 for a ticket whatever
it cost to go to the movies these days to

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:12.750
sit in the movie theater to watch this thing
that you created.

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:21.780
I mean imagine somebody stops you in the street
and says “I’ve got to tell you about this

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:23.710
dream that I had.

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:27.120
I had this dream and I’ve got to tell you
about it.”

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:34.930
So let’s say you’re a generous and a humane
person and you say “Okay, let’s hear it!”

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:38.330
And imagine that the person…I mean you’ve
wanted to tell the experience that you’ve

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:42.099
wanted to tell a dream that you had to somebody
and there are people who surely say “I have

00:04:42.099 --> 00:04:44.380
to tell you about this dream I had.”

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:49.500
What about just before they tell you the dream
they say “Before I do this, you have to

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:52.720
meet two conditions.

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:55.900
You have to be ready to sit there for two
hours, I’m going to need two hours to tell

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:58.520
you this dream.”

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:00.379
I mean that would stop me right there.

00:05:00.379 --> 00:05:03.449
But that’s not the end of it, that’s just
the beginning of it.

00:05:03.449 --> 00:05:06.650
And the other part is “I also want $15.00”
or whatever a ticket costs.

00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:08.230
I mean it’s crazy.

00:05:08.230 --> 00:05:09.880
And I said to this person “No!

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:11.990
Don’t do it.”

00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:17.820
If you are wondering about it, if you have
to ask about it, then you shouldn’t do it.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:20.979
I’m not about to say to somebody “Give
us a chance!

00:05:20.979 --> 00:05:22.110
Give us a chance.

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:23.849
Please be a writer.”

00:05:23.849 --> 00:05:29.189
There’s nothing in it for the writer to
tell them that.

00:05:29.189 --> 00:05:39.860
And the same thing with on a narrower basis
with a story whether this is something they

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:44.030
should write a story about.

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:45.289
I go to a lot of writers conferences.

00:05:45.289 --> 00:05:50.000
I am an academic but I don’t go to a lot
of scholarly conferences.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:56.000
But I do go to bunches of writer conferences
and I was at one recently in Northern California

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:01.360
and there were a lot of agents there on panels
and I was chatting with them.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:08.560
I was a presenter as they were and I remember
them complaining to me (the agents) that writers

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:11.919
were coming up to them at the conference and
saying “What should I write about?

00:06:11.919 --> 00:06:12.919
What’s hot now?

00:06:12.919 --> 00:06:14.419
What should I look for?”

00:06:14.419 --> 00:06:18.160
Imagine asking somebody else what to write
about.

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:22.669
I mean if the writer doesn’t care about
what she’s writing about, why would the

00:06:22.669 --> 00:06:23.669
agent care?

00:06:23.669 --> 00:06:25.539
Why would the producer care?

00:06:25.539 --> 00:06:29.569
Why would audiences care?

00:06:29.569 --> 00:06:36.110
So again you really have to…you can’t
look to anybody else other than yourself in

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:37.050
this enterprise.

